{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 all three groups sit in ONE superfamily: class a (All alpha proteins) > fold a.25 (Ferritin-like) > superfamily a.25.1 (Ferritin-like, SUNID 47240). They are separate FAMILIES under it: a.25.1.1 Ferritin (SUNID 47241), a.25.1.2 Ribonucleotide reductase-like (SUNID 47253; includes the protein 'Ribonucleotide reductase R2', a.25.1.2), and a.25.1.3 Manganese catalase (T-catalase) (SUNID 100951). Other families in the same superfamily include YciF-like, half-ferritin, PMT1231-like, MiaE-like, AMB4284-like, Rv2844-like, and 'automated matches'. The superfamily carries the SCOPe comment 'contains bimetal-ion centre in the middle of the bundle', and the homology response has homology_implied_by_level=true with scope sf and no warnings or heterogeneity entries.

Interpretation: shared superfamily is SCOPe's positive assertion of probable common ancestry (a shared four-helix-bundle scaffold with a central dinuclear metal site). The different families mean that the relationships are more remote than within-family relationships. So the three are best read as remote homologs that diverged in chemistry from a common bundle scaffold: ferritin is a ferroxidase/iron-storage protein, RNR R2 is a diiron-tyrosyl-radical oxygen-activating enzyme for nucleotide reduction, and Mn catalase is a dimanganese H2O2-disproportionation enzyme. This is an example of structural and ancestral conservation with functional and chemical divergence. The chemistry descriptions come from background knowledge, not from SCOPe, which only provides the hierarchy and the bimetal-centre comment.

Limits: superfamily membership supports common ancestry but does not support transferring function, substrate, metal identity (Fe vs Mn), redox chemistry, mechanism, or oligomeric assembly between families. A ferritin structure is not a valid template for catalase or RNR activity, and vice versa. Also, SCOPe family placement is not proof of any particular evolutionary path or of which chemistry is ancestral. Note that the fold (a.25) is a broader structural grouping that on its own would not imply ancestry; here the superfamily-level assertion is what supports the homology claim. 'Ribonucleotide reductase' appears elsewhere in SCOPe in unrelated lineages (R1 subunit a.98.1 and c.7.1; class III NRDD), so only the R2 'ribonucleotide reductase-like' family belongs to this superfamily. I did not retrieve individual domain-level lineages, sequence or structure alignments, or historical releases, so statements about specific domains, residue-level metal ligand conservation, or reclassification history are not verified.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ferritin&mode=token&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=manganese+catalase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=ribonucleotide+reductase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/47240/children?limit=20"]}]}
